-- glycerol, the case where the correct count depends on the medium. Its central carbon carries a
-- hydrogen, a hydroxyl and two identical arms, so the molecule is achiral and its only symmetry
-- operation is the reflection that exchanges the two arms. Three groups act on it and give three
-- different counts. The bond graph exchanges the arms and also the two hydrogens within each methylene.
-- The configured molecule in an achiral solvent is indistinguishable from its mirror image, so the arm
-- exchange counts and the within-methylene exchange does not: the arms are enantiotopic, the methylene
-- hydrogens diastereotopic. The configured molecule in a chiral solvent has no symmetry at all, and
-- every hydrogen is its own class. This is the pair of groups that the field distinguishes by adding a
-- chiral agent, and the same distinction the enzyme makes: glycerol kinase phosphorylates one arm and
-- not the other.
